---
// The Settings page of the app window, working: pick a profile in the strip and change its settings, or
// General. Choices are remembered in this browser and show up across the site (icon style in the Dock,
// cards and launcher; profile colour; the Claude open dot; how long a Dock click shows the usage; the menu bar icons).
// The server renders the "home" profile; src/pages/settings.astro fills the controls in.
// A profile's page is the app's layout A (26.11.0): one card per part, each with its title and ⓘ, rows with the label
// on the left (one width, so the values line up) and an ⓘ on every label. On/off is the lamp switch and a few words
// that follow it; 2–3 choices are segments, more a pop-up, pictures tiles under the label; at most one grey line
// under a row. A part that's off folds its card to its switch row.
//   Profile, Claude Code, Claude window, Alerts | Dock icon | Menu bar (in two columns Menu bar goes under the first).
//   General: cl_audit (Usage in the background, Privacy, About) · How usage looks (Face, Dashboard cards, Widget) ·
//            The Dock (App icon, Dock), each column with its subject printed at the top.
// Three columns from 1180 px; two from 800 (the third column goes under the first; settings.astro moves it); one below.
// Profile tiles can be dragged along the strip to change the profiles' order (settings.astro).
import { accounts } from "../data/demo";
import { colors, tileColors, styles, iconSrc, laneSrc, refreshChoices, dockRunsChoices, menuBarFamilies, appIconStyles, appIconSrc, appIconDefault } from "../data/prefs";
import { limitsHTML, designs } from "../data/panelView";
import { lane as mbLane } from "../data/menubar";
import { version, build, changelog } from "../data/site";
import AccIcon from "./AccIcon.astro";
import ExtLink from "./ExtLink.astro";
import SessionsPages from "./SessionsPages.astro";
import SwitchPage from "./SwitchPage.astro";
import Experimental from "./Experimental.astro";

const a = accounts[0];
const panels = [["glow", "Glow"], ["rings", "Rings"], ["numbers", "Numbers"], ["segments", "Segments"], ["pace", "Pace"]];
const swatchBg = (hex: string) => (hex === "usage" ? "conic-gradient(#22c55e 0 33%, #f59e0b 0 66%, #ef4444 0)" : hex);
const mbColours = [["levels", "Levels"], ["system", "System"], ["accent", "Accent"], ["profile", "Profile colour"]];
const mbStyles = menuBarFamilies.flatMap(([, list]) => list);
const widgetPlates = [["system", "System"], ["std", "Std"], ["black", "Black"], ["silver", "Silver"]];
// the Dock icon's Preview strip: not running, then as the limit fills up, full with its countdown (as the app's)
const dockStrip: [string, string][] = [["not running", "x"], ["plenty left", "0"], ["half used", "2"], ["at limit", "3"], ["full", "full"]];
// a switch and the words that follow its state (settings.astro fills them in)
const sw = (key: string, label: string, on = true) => `<span class="ctl-pair"><button type="button" class="mswitch${on ? " on" : ""}" role="switch" aria-checked="${on}" data-switch="${key}" aria-label="${label}"></button><span class="sw-t" data-sw-t="${key}"></span></span>`;
---
<div class="settings" id="settings" data-acc={a.id}>
  <div class="strip">
    <button type="button" class="tile tile-plain" data-select-acc="general"><span class="tile-text"><b>General</b><span class="sub">cl_audit · looks · widget · Dock</span></span></button>
    {accounts.map((x, i) => (
      <button type="button" class:list={["tile", { sel: i === 0 }]} data-select-acc={x.id} draggable="true" title="Drag left or right to change the profiles' order">
        <AccIcon id={x.id} src={x.icon} size={34} />
        <span class="tile-text"><b data-acc-name={x.id}>{x.name}</b><span class="sub" data-tile-sub={x.id}>{x.dir}</span></span>
      </button>
    ))}
    <a class="tile tile-plus" href="/install/" title="New profile (see Install)" aria-label="New profile">+</a>
    <span class="spacer"></span>
    <!-- whether everything is saved, as the app's top strip says it -->
    <span class="disp fixed s-status" data-status>Saved <em>· as you change</em></span>
  </div>

  <!-- A profile's settings -->
  <div class="s-cols" data-profile-view>
    <div class="s-col" data-pcol="1">
      <div class="card c-p"><h4>Profile <i class="idot" title="Who this profile is in cl_audit: its name, the label on its icons, its colour, and whether Fable's own weekly limit shows.">i</i></h4>
        <label class="srow erow vcol"><span>Name <i class="idot" title="What cl_audit calls this profile; its Dock icon app is “cl_audit <name>”. Saved when you press Return or click elsewhere (Esc puts it back); saving restarts that Dock icon.">i</i></span><span class="fcol"><input class="field" data-field="name" value={a.name} maxlength="20" /><span class="fmsg" data-msg="name" aria-live="polite"></span></span></label>
        <label class="srow erow vcol"><span>Icon label <i class="idot" title="Up to 6 letters printed on this profile's Dock icon and menu bar icon, like MAIN or WORK, so you can tell the icons apart. Saved when you press Return or click elsewhere (Esc puts it back).">i</i></span><span class="fcol"><input class="field" data-field="label" value="HOME" maxlength="6" /><span class="fmsg" data-msg="label" aria-live="polite"></span></span></label>
        <div class="srow"><span>Colour <i class="idot" title="This profile's colour inside cl_audit: the dot by its name on the dashboard and in the launcher, its Show key, its share of the history charts and the dot on its usage panel. The Dock icon's own colour is under Icon.">i</i></span><span class="swatches" data-swatches="color">{Object.entries(colors).map(([k, hex]) => <button type="button" data-color={k} class:list={{ on: k === "orange" }} style={`background:${hex}`} aria-label={k} title={k} />)}</span></div>
        <div class="srow" data-fable-row><span>Fable's limit <i class="idot" title="Fable's own weekly limit (Max and Ultra plans, premium Team and Enterprise seats): shown on this profile's dashboard card, usage panels, Dock and menu bar menus and the widget, with its alerts. Off: left out everywhere; it still counts in Anthropic's limits.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("showFable", "Fable's limit")} /></div>
        <div class="srow end"><button type="button" class="danger linkish" data-remove>Remove profile…</button></div>
      </div>

      <div class="card c-c"><h4>Claude Code <i class="idot" title="The Claude Code folder this profile uses and its sign-in. Its Claude window comes with it: the usual Claude app for the profile using ~/.claude, its own Claude window (own sign-in, chats and settings) for any other.">i</i></h4>
        <!-- As the app (26.11.18): Accounts first, one box per account (IN USE, STANDBY: no limits), the key Sign in with
             another account… (accounts aren't added by hand: those found on this Mac list themselves), Folders from before; then
             Folder and (any folder but ~/.claude) Share from and Shared, read-only. Every row stacks, label above and
             the value whole below, so long addresses and paths are never cut. -->
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Accounts <i class="idot" title="The claude.ai accounts this profile has. Its Claude Code folder holds one sign-in at a time: IN USE is the account signed in there, and the only one whose limits cl_audit reads. The others are STANDBY: signed out, with no folder of their own, so they show no limits, only when they were last in use. They are found on this Mac by themselves: the Claude window's sessions folders, Claude Code's records and their backups, and the transcripts; Forget… takes one off. Switching to one (the Switch page, experimental: it can lose data) is meant to move the Claude window's Code sessions and scheduled tasks to it inside this profile's folder, with a backup first and Undo, then signs Claude Code in again on the same folder as that account; the account it leaves goes on standby. Whatever account signs in to the folder, even from Terminal, is the one in use: cl_audit follows it at once. An account belongs to one profile. cl_audit's background agent reads the sign-in from the Keychain, read-only, the same way Claude Code does; no new Keychain permission, and no sign-in is ever copied. cl_audit itself never reads or writes Keychain items during a switch; Claude Code's own sign-in updates its own item.

Renew sign-in (in the account in use's pop-up): Claude Code's sign-in uses a short-lived access key, which Claude Code swaps for a fresh one whenever it runs. cl_audit only reads that key, so after a while without Claude Code it runs out and the usage stops updating. On: cl_audit then runs Claude Code once in the background (a one-line prompt, “Reply with OK.”, on Haiku, nothing saved) so it swaps the key; at most every 30 min, using a sliver of this profile's usage. Off: the usage waits until you next use Claude Code.">i</i></span>
          <div class="acc-list">
            <!-- IN USE: a lit lamp, the address as a pop-up (✓ it, Log in…, Renew sign-in by itself), its plan, the
                 limits in the dashboard's card style -->
            <div class="abox in-use">
              <div class="abox-head"><i class="abox-lamp on"></i>
                <span class="abox-t login-ctl">
                  <button type="button" class="abox-title" data-login-btn aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false"><span class="email" data-text="email">{a.email}</span><span class="chev" aria-hidden="true">⌃⌄</span></button>
                  <span class="login-menu" data-login-menu role="menu" hidden></span>
                  <small data-abox-detail>{a.plan}</small>
                </span>
                <span class="disp xs abox-badge"><span class="g">In use</span></span></div>
              <div class="abox-limits" data-abox-limits></div>
            </div>
            <!-- the standby accounts, unlit, in the order a full limit offers them: each shows its address (a pop-up: Switch
                 to it…, Forget…), plan, Standby and when it was last in use here. Click one to select it (again to
                 deselect); its Standby display becomes a Switch account key, which opens the Switch page with it picked -->
            <div class="acc-pick" data-standby-boxes></div>
            <p class="g-note sub" data-pick-hint hidden>Click a standby account to select it, then Switch account.</p>
            <!-- the accounts found on this Mac are listed by themselves; a brand-new one signs in from here -->
            <div class="add-acc">
              <button type="button" class="key k" data-sign-other>Sign in with another account…</button>
              <small class="sub">Accounts found on this Mac are listed here by themselves.</small>
            </div>
            <!-- the folders an earlier cl_audit made for this profile's accounts: Move to Trash… or Keep it -->
            <div class="leftovers" data-leftovers hidden></div>
          </div></div>
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Folder <i class="idot" title="The Claude Code folder for this profile, like ~/.claude: its sign-in, settings and history live there. One profile per folder; folders are used where they are (Claude Code names a folder's Keychain sign-in after its path).">i</i></span><span class="folder-ctl"><select class="menu mono" data-folder aria-label="Folder"></select><span class="folder-btn" title="Choose another folder…" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="15" height="12" viewBox="0 0 15 12"><path d="M1 2.2C1 1.5 1.5 1 2.2 1h3.3l1.4 1.5h5.9c.7 0 1.2.5 1.2 1.2v6.1c0 .7-.5 1.2-1.2 1.2H2.2C1.5 11 1 10.5 1 9.8z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/></svg></span></span></div>
        <div data-share hidden>
          <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Share from <i class="idot" title="The Claude Code folder this profile's folder links some items from (instructions, agents, skills, commands, memory & history), so edits there show up here too. Sign-in, plugins and usage stay separate.">i</i></span><span class="share-v mono" data-share-from></span></div>
          <div class="srow vcol stk" data-shared-row><span>Shared <i class="idot" title="The items that are links into the folder above; the rest are this folder's own. cl_audit makes these links when it creates a folder (New profile) and doesn't change them afterwards.">i</i></span><span class="share-v" data-shared></span></div>
          <p class="g-note sub rline" data-share-line></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Sessions (switch UX round 2): what's kept, its backups, Export and Import. A backup is a quick copy on this Mac
           (clones, seconds, almost no space); an export is a file. One format; Import restores the way a backup does.
           The keys open their pages in place of the profile's settings, like the Switch page. Sample figures only. -->
      <div class="card c-c c-s"><h4>Sessions <i class="idot" title="This profile's Code sessions: Claude Code's in its folder, and the Claude window's Code tab with its scheduled tasks and sidebar. Backups and Export keep copies of them; Restore and Import bring them back. A sign-in is never in a copy.">i</i></h4>
        <Experimental />
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Kept here <i class="idot" title="Claude Code's session transcripts, the entries of the Claude window's Code tab (for every account it has used), and how much room they take. The last sentence says when Claude Code deletes them; since Claude Code 2.1.248 that depends on where the session ran (see Keep for).">i</i></span><span class="v-soft"><b>310</b> in Claude Code · <b>17</b> in the Claude window (14 in the sidebar) · 5.6 GB · since 6 Oct. Claude Code deletes terminal sessions 30 days after their last use; ones from the Claude window it keeps (Claude Code 2.1.248 and later).</span></div>
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Keep for <i class="idot" title="How long Claude Code keeps the sessions you ran in the terminal: cleanupPeriodDays in ~/.claude/settings.json (default 30), kept with every other setting in that file. Sessions started or last continued in the Claude window are kept at any age (Claude Code 2.1.248 and later), unless desktopSessionCleanupPeriodDays gives them a limit or you deleted or archived the session in the Claude window. Your organisation's managed settings (or an error in them), HIPAA and zero data retention make the period apply to them too; cl_audit can't tell whether HIPAA or zero data retention applies to your account. A longer time keeps the terminal sessions available to claude --resume and for backups; it can't bring back what is already deleted.">i</i></span><select class="menu" aria-label="Keep for"><option>30 days (Claude Code's default)</option><option>90 days</option><option>A year</option></select></div>
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Backups <i class="idot" title="Quick copies on this Mac: one is made whenever a switch moves the sessions, and another when you press Back up now. They're clones, so they share space with the originals. Restore puts back what's missing and is built not to overwrite.">i</i></span>
          <div class="bk-list">
            <div data-bk-list></div>
            <div class="bk-row"><button type="button" class="key k" data-bk-now>Back up now</button><small class="ap-note"><span data-pn>work</span>'s window is open: close it first, or the Code-tab list and sidebar records are left out.</small></div>
            <small class="ap-note">⋯ holds Show in Finder and Delete. The last 3 are kept, plus any made in the last 30 days.</small>
          </div></div>
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Export <i class="idot" title="This profile's sessions as one file, for another Mac or for keeping: a zip (Finder opens it), or an encrypted disk image when it keeps transcripts that hold keys. Made from a fresh copy or from a backup, never with a sign-in. Accounts cl_audit knows go in only as fingerprints; an address typed into a conversation stays as written. The same format as a backup, so Import restores it by the same rules.">i</i></span><span class="bk-row"><button type="button" class="key k" data-sess-go="export">Export…</button><small class="ap-note">Never exported. A file for another Mac or for keeping.</small></span></div>
        <div class="srow vcol stk"><span>Import <i class="idot" title="Puts an export's sessions into this profile: the file is checked and unpacked as a backup first, then restored from: the same rules as Restore. It only adds what is missing and is built not to overwrite anything. An export of this profile, of another profile or of another Mac. Undo last import is meant to take back what it added; it may not bring everything back.">i</i></span><span class="bk-row"><button type="button" class="key k" data-sess-go="import">Import…</button><small class="ap-note">From an export of this profile, another profile or another Mac.</small></span></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Claude window: its switch first, then whether it goes with the Dock icon and what may open it. Off, the card
           folds to the switch row. -->
      <div class="card c-w"><h4>Claude window <i class="idot" title="This profile's Claude window: its own (own sign-in, chats and settings), or the usual Claude app for ~/.claude. Whether it goes with the Dock icon and what may open it; the cl_audit agent does this, whether or not cl_audit is open.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow"><span>Claude window <i class="idot" title="On: a Claude window signed in to this profile's account; open it from the dashboard, the Dock icon or the menu bar icon. Off: none, and nothing opens one; its folder and sign-in are kept, so turning it back on needs no new sign-in.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("win", "Claude window")} /></div>
        <div class="fold" data-cw-body>
          <div class="srow"><span>With Dock icon <i class="idot" title="On: quitting this profile's Dock icon quits its window too (Claude can ask about unsent work first; the Dock icon's menu then has “Quit and keep Claude window”), and, with Opens from ▸ Dock click too, starting the Dock icon (a click on its Dock tile, or its switch on the dashboard) opens the window. Not at login.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("windowFollows", "With Dock icon", false)} /></div>
          <p class="g-note sub rline" data-wf-line hidden>No Dock icon to go with: Usage in the Dock is off.</p>
          <div class="srow vcol"><span>Opens from <i class="idot" title="Dock click too: a click on the Dock icon opens the window as its Click says, and so does starting the Dock icon with With Dock icon on. Only when asked: a Dock click shows the usage only; the dashboard's Open window key and tile, Open Claude in the Dock icon's and the menu bar icon's menus, and the details panel open it.">i</i></span><select class="menu" data-field="opensFromDock" aria-label="Opens from"><option value="1">Dock click too</option><option value="0">Only when asked</option></select></div>
          <p class="g-note sub rline" data-of-line hidden>A Dock click shows usage only; Open window and the Dock and menu bar menus open it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card c-u"><h4>Alerts <i class="idot" title="How often this profile's usage is read, and its Mac notifications: for the 5-hour, weekly and Fable limits, and for usage credits when they start and run out.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow vcol"><span>Refresh every <i class="idot" title="How often cl_audit asks Anthropic for this profile's usage, with or without its Dock icon. Shorter is fresher; each check is one small request.">i</i></span><select class="menu" data-field="refresh" aria-label="Refresh every">{refreshChoices.map((o) => <option>{o}</option>)}</select></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Notify at <i class="idot" title="A Mac notification when this profile's 5-hour, weekly or Fable limit reaches this level, when one is full (with when it's back), and when the profile starts spending usage credits or runs out of them. Off: no notifications.">i</i></span><span class="ctl-pair"><button type="button" class="mswitch on" role="switch" aria-checked="true" data-switch="notify" aria-label="Notify at"></button><span class="stepper" data-notify-step><button type="button" data-step="notifyAt" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Lower">−</button><span data-value="notifyAt">90%</span><button type="button" data-step="notifyAt" data-dir="1" aria-label="Higher">＋</button></span><span class="sw-t" data-sw-t="notify"></span></span></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Notify when back <i class="idot" title="One Mac notification when this profile's full 5-hour or weekly limit resets, so you know it works again (“Claude home is back”). Only for a limit that was full; while the other limit is still full, it waits for that one.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("notifyBack", "Notify when back")} /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="s-col" data-pcol="2">
      <!-- Dock icon: everything about it, from whether it's there to its look. Off, the card folds to its switch row. -->
      <div class="card c-d"><h4>Dock icon <i class="idot" title="Everything about this profile's Dock icon: whether it's there, when it runs, what a click on it does, its usage panel and its look. The cl_audit agent starts and quits it, whether or not cl_audit is open; starting or quitting it never stops the usage, which cl_audit reads for every profile, always. Double-click or ⌥-click always opens the large panel.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow"><span>Usage in the Dock <i class="idot" title="On: a Dock icon shows this profile's usage; click it for details. Off: no Dock icon: it quits, is unpinned and its app is removed; the usage still shows on the dashboard, in the launcher and in alerts, and turning it back on brings the icon back to its place, with the same look and click settings. Changing it restarts the Dock once.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("dock", "Usage in the Dock")} /></div>
        <div class="fold" data-dk-body>
          <div class="srow vcol"><span>Runs <i class="idot" title="At login: always there, from when you log in to the Mac. With its window: starts when this profile's Claude window opens and quits 10 s after it quits (a Claude update restarts the window). When started: only when you start it (a click on its Dock tile, or its switch on the dashboard).">i</i></span><select class="menu" data-field="dockRuns" aria-label="Runs">{dockRunsChoices.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select></div>
          <p class="g-note sub rline" data-runs-line></p>
          <div class="srow vcol"><span>Click <i class="idot" title="Usage, then Claude: the usage panel for a moment, then the Claude window. Open Claude: the Claude window straight away. Details panel: the large usage panel, and Claude only when you ask. Without a Claude window, or with Claude window ▸ Opens from ▸ Only when asked, a click shows the usage only: Usage panel (briefly) or Details panel.">i</i></span><select class="menu" data-click aria-label="Click"></select></div>
          <p class="g-note sub rline" data-click-line hidden></p>
          <div class="srow" data-peekrow><span>Usage shows for <i class="idot" data-peek-info title="How long the usage panel stays up after a click, before the Claude window opens.">i</i></span><span class="stepper"><button type="button" data-step="peek" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Shorter">−</button><span data-value="peek">3 s</span><button type="button" data-step="peek" data-dir="1" aria-label="Longer">＋</button></span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Press and hold <i class="idot" title="Press and hold the icon for its details panel and a short menu. Double-click or ⌥-click also opens the panel; right-click shows the full menu.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("hold", "Press and hold")} /></div>
          <div class="srow stacked"><span>Usage panel <i class="idot" title="How the usage panel looks when you click or press and hold this Dock icon. The dashboard's cards have one style for all profiles, in General.">i</i></span>
            <!-- as General ▸ Card style: the keys, then one large preview of the chosen panel's limits (build 189) -->
            <div class="design-pane">
              <span class="switch small-switch" role="group" aria-label="Usage panel">{panels.map(([k, n]) => <button type="button" data-panel={k} class:list={{ on: k === "segments" }}>{n}</button>)}</span>
              <div class="card-prev" data-panel-prev set:html={limitsHTML("home", "segments", false)} />
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="srow stacked"><span>Icon <i class="idot" title="How this profile's Dock icon draws the usage: numbers, rings, the panels' looks, or tiles in the level colours.">i</i></span>
            <div class="gallery g5">
              {styles.map(([k, n]) => (
                <button type="button" class:list={["g-tile", { on: k === "bars" }]} data-style={k}>
                  <AccIcon id="home" src={iconSrc("home", k)} size={44} loading="lazy" gallery={k} /><span>{n}</span>
                </button>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="srow sw-row"><span>Colour <i class="idot" title="The colour of this profile's Dock icon: its label and tiles. By usage (the last swatch): it follows the 5-hour level, green to red. The profile's colour inside cl_audit is under Profile.">i</i></span>
            <span class="swatches" data-swatches="iconColor">{Object.entries(tileColors).map(([k, hex]) => <button type="button" data-icon-color={k} style={`background:${swatchBg(hex)}`} aria-label={k === "usage" ? "By usage" : k} title={k === "usage" ? "By usage" : k} />)}</span></div>
          <div class="srow sw-row"><span>Frame <i class="idot" title="A thin ring in this colour round the Dock icon, to spot it quickly among the others. The crossed circle: no ring.">i</i></span>
            <span class="swatches" data-swatches="frame"><button type="button" data-frame="" class="none-sw" aria-label="No frame" title="No frame"></button>{Object.entries(tileColors).map(([k, hex]) => <button type="button" data-frame={k} style={`background:${swatchBg(hex)}`} aria-label={k === "usage" ? "By usage" : k} title={k === "usage" ? "By usage" : k} />)}</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Claude open dot <i class="idot" title="An orange dot at the Dock icon's top-left corner while this profile's Claude window is open.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("dot", "Claude open dot")} /></div>
          <div class="pv-strip">
            <div class="lane-head"><b>Preview</b> <span class="sub">not running, then as the limit fills up</span></div>
            <div class="pv-row">{dockStrip.map(([t, step]) => (
              <span class="pv-item"><AccIcon id="home" src={step === "full" ? "/img/acc/lane-home-full.png" : laneSrc("home", "bars", step)} size={38} lane={step} nodot={step === "x"} /><span>{t}</span></span>
            ))}</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="s-col" data-pcol="3">
      <!-- Menu bar (26.11.0): the profile's own icon in the menu bar. The site's own menu bar shows it
           (layouts/Desktop.astro), with the profile's menu. Off, the card folds to its switch row. -->
      <div class="card c-m"><h4>Menu bar <i class="idot" title="An icon for this profile in the menu bar, like its Dock icon: drawn by the cl_audit agent, so it works with or without the Dock icon. Its menu has the limits, Open Claude window, Refresh now and Profile settings. ⌘-drag it to move it; macOS remembers where. (A demo: it shows in this site's menu bar.)">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow"><span>In the menu bar <i class="idot" title="On: an icon for this profile in the menu bar. Off: none; the usage is read either way.">i</i></span><Fragment set:html={sw("mbOn", "In the menu bar", false)} /></div>
        <div class="fold" data-mb-body>
          <div class="srow stacked"><span>Style <i class="idot" title="How the icon draws the usage: two bars (the 5-hour limit over the week), a ring (the week outside, the 5-hour limit inside), with or without the fuller limit as a number, or just numbers.">i</i></span>
            <div class="mb-tiles">{mbStyles.map(([k, t]) => <button type="button" class="mb-st" data-mb-style={k} title={t}><span class="mb-chip dark" data-mb-glyph={k}></span><span>{t}</span></button>)}</div></div>
          <div class="srow stacked"><span>Colours <i class="idot" title="Levels: green, amber, red as the limits fill up. System: the menu bar's own colour, like Apple's icons (a full limit shows by its countdown, as macOS doesn't colour these icons). Accent: your Mac's accent colour, following it when it changes. Profile colour: this profile's colour.">i</i></span>
            <div class="mb-tiles">{mbColours.map(([k, t]) => <button type="button" class="mb-st" data-mb-colours={k}><span class="mb-chip dark" data-mb-cglyph={k}></span><span>{t}</span></button>)}</div></div>
          <div class="srow seg-row"><span>Label <i class="idot" title="The profile's label before its icon (“MAIN”), or none.">i</i></span>
            <span class="switch small-switch" role="group" aria-label="Label"><button type="button" data-mb-label="0">None</button><button type="button" data-mb-label="1">Profile label</button></span></div>
          <div class="srow seg-row"><span>When full <i class="idot" title="While a full limit holds the profile back: its countdown beside the icon (“1h 42m”), or the icon in red only. With the System colours it's always the countdown.">i</i></span>
            <span class="switch small-switch" role="group" aria-label="When full"><button type="button" data-mb-full="countdown">Countdown</button><button type="button" data-mb-full="red">Red only</button></span></div>
          <div class="pv-strip">
            <div class="lane-head"><b>Preview</b> <span class="sub">your menu bar (dark), then as the limit fills up</span></div>
            <div class="mb-lane dark" data-mb-lane="dark">{mbLane.map(() => <span class="mb-chip" />)}</div>
            <div class="mb-caps">{mbLane.map(([t]) => <span>{t}</span>)}</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- the pages a profile's Sessions card opens, and its Switch page, in place of its settings -->
  <SessionsPages />
  <SwitchPage />

  <!-- General settings (26.11.0): cl_audit (Usage in the background, Privacy, About) · How usage looks (Face, Dashboard
       cards, Widget) · The Dock (App icon, Dock). In two columns The Dock goes under cl_audit (settings.astro moves it);
       one column keeps the three-column order. Every row is one line. -->
  <div class="g-grid" data-general-view hidden>
    <div class="g-col" data-gcol="1">
      <div class="colhead">cl_audit</div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Usage in the background <i class="idot" title="cl_audit's background agent reads every profile's usage on each profile's own schedule, renews sign-ins, sends the alerts, and starts and quits Dock icons and Claude windows as each profile says. macOS starts it when you log in and again after a crash; cl_audit starts it too when this window opens and whenever it has been silent for 90 s. Shows as “cl_audit Agent” in Activity Monitor; macOS restarts it if it's force-quit. To stop it, turn cl_audit off in System Settings ▸ General ▸ Login Items &amp; Extensions.">i</i></h4>
        <span class="disp fill ub-disp" data-usage-disp><span class="g">Reading usage</span> <em>· 3 profiles · every 5–10 min</em></span>
        <p class="g-note">cl_audit reads every profile's usage and sends the alerts on its own, even with this window and the Dock icons closed. It starts when you log in.</p>
        <div class="srow"><span>Alerts <i class="idot" title="Alerts come from cl_audit when a limit reaches the level set for each profile. macOS asks once; you can change it in System Settings ▸ Notifications ▸ cl_audit.">i</i></span><span class="disp"><span class="g">Allowed</span></span></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Last read</span><span class="v-soft">1 min ago</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Privacy <i class="idot" title="What cl_audit shows and what it reads on this Mac.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow"><span>Show emails <i class="idot" title="Off: email addresses are left out of the dashboard, the launcher, the usage panels and the Dock icon menus, and blurred in Settings (hover one to see it). For screen sharing and screenshots.">i</i></span><span class="ctl-pair"><button type="button" class="mswitch on" role="switch" aria-checked="true" data-toggle-emails aria-label="Show emails"></button><span class="sw-t" data-emails-t>shown</span></span></div>
        <!-- 26.11.0: whether cl_audit reads Claude Code's session files at all (off: the dashboard's history is hidden) -->
        <div class="srow"><span>Usage history <i class="idot" title="The dashboard's history (when you use Claude, tokens per day, where they go), the cost estimate, the CSV export and the launcher's 7-day row. Read from the session files Claude Code keeps on this Mac: only the time, model, token counts, project folder and surface of each reply, never the messages. Off: no session file is read, and those are hidden. The limits come from Anthropic either way.">i</i></span><span class="ctl-pair"><button type="button" class="mswitch on" role="switch" aria-checked="true" data-toggle-history aria-label="Usage history"></button><span class="sw-t" data-history-t>tokens, cost, CSV</span></span></div>
      </div>
      <!-- Backups (26.11.1): where a profile's session backups go and how many are kept -->
      <div class="card" data-gcard="backups"><h4>Backups <i class="idot" title="A backup is a quick copy of a profile's sessions on this Mac, made by every switch that moves them and by Back up now in the profile's Sessions card. It never holds a sign-in. Here: the folder they go to, and how many are kept. Backups already made stay where they were if you change the folder.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow stacked"><span>Where <i class="idot" title="The folder backups are made in: one folder per profile, one per backup. Clones need the same volume as the Claude window's data; another volume gets full copies, which take longer and more room.">i</i></span>
          <span class="v-soft">cl_audit's folder ▸ backups</span>
          <span class="bk-row"><button type="button" class="key k" data-bk-demo="Change…">Change…</button><button type="button" class="key k" data-bk-demo="Show in Finder">Show in Finder</button></span></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Keep the last <i class="idot" title="The newest backups of each profile that are always kept, however old.">i</i></span><span class="stepper"><button type="button" data-bk-step="last" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Fewer">−</button><span data-bk-value="last">3</span><button type="button" data-bk-step="last" data-dir="1" aria-label="More">＋</button></span></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>And any under <i class="idot" title="Backups made within this many days are kept too. After a switch's backup is made (and after Back up now), the ones past both are deleted; one an unfinished switch names is never deleted. 0 keeps only the last few.">i</i></span><span class="stepper"><button type="button" data-bk-step="days" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Fewer days">−</button><span data-bk-value="days">30 days</span><button type="button" data-bk-step="days" data-dir="1" aria-label="More days">＋</button></span></div>
        <p class="g-note sub" data-bk-line>The last 3 of each profile, and any under 30 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" data-gcard="about"><h4>About</h4>
        <div class="srow"><span>Version <i class="idot" title="Calendar version: the year and month of the release, then a counter within that month (26.10.1 = the second release in October 2026). The build counts every change.">i</i></span><span class="disp">{version} <em>· build {build}</em></span></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Changes</span><ExtLink class="key k" href={changelog}>What's new</ExtLink></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Clean-up <i class="idot" title="The agent tidies up on its own: at login, after every change and every 6 hours. It removes only what is provably cl_audit's: Dock icon apps no profile wants, second copies, what macOS keeps for them, saved usage of removed profiles, Dock pins of apps that are gone and old Terminal scripts. Never a Claude Code folder, a Claude window's data or a sign-in.">i</i></span><span class="v-soft one" data-cleanup>Cleaned up 2 leftovers · 09:00</span></div>
        <div class="srow"><span>Uninstall <i class="idot" title="Removes every Dock icon app and its pin, the agent, saved usage, settings and caches, then moves cl_audit to the Trash. You choose per profile whether its Claude Code folder (signed out first) and its own Claude window's data go too; ~/.claude and the standard window are never deleted.">i</i></span><button type="button" class="key k danger-key" data-uninstall>Uninstall cl_audit…</button></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="g-col" data-gcol="2">
      <div class="colhead">How usage looks</div>
      <!-- the same face as the title bar's keys (Desktop.astro handles every .fk) -->
      <div class="card"><h4>Face</h4>
        <div class="facecards">
          <div class="fcard" data-face-card="std"><div class="fpic" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#29292b,#202022)"><i style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#74746f,#52524e)"></i><b style="background:#0a1113"></b><u></u></div><button type="button" class="fk" data-face="std" aria-pressed="true">Std</button></div>
          <div class="fcard" data-face-card="black"><div class="fpic" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#1d1d1f,#111113)"><i style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#74746f,#52524e)"></i><b style="background:#060809"></b><u></u></div><button type="button" class="fk" data-face="black" aria-pressed="false">Black</button></div>
          <div class="fcard" data-face-card="silver"><div class="fpic" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#e0e2e3,#c6c8c9)"><i style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#ededeb,#c8c8c3)"></i><b style="background:#f6f7f8"></b><u></u></div><button type="button" class="fk" data-face="silver" aria-pressed="false">Silver</button></div>
        </div>
        <p class="g-note">The look of this window, its settings and the dashboard. Silver is a light face: the window takes the Mac's light appearance. The Dock icons keep their own designs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Dashboard cards <i class="idot" title="How the 5-hour and weekly limits look on every profile's dashboard card. Each profile's own usage panel has its design in that profile's settings.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow stacked"><span>Card style</span>
          <div class="design-pane">
            <span class="switch small-switch" role="group" aria-label="Card style">{designs.map(([k, n]) => <button type="button" data-card-style={k} class:list={{ on: k === "segments" }}>{n}</button>)}</span>
            <div class="card-prev" data-card-prev set:html={limitsHTML("sample", "segments", false)} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- Widget (26.11.0): how the "Claude usage" widget draws the limits, with the medium widget as the preview, then its look -->
      <div class="card"><h4>Widget <i class="idot" title="The “Claude usage” widget for the desktop or Notification Center (macOS 14 or later): click the date in the menu bar, then Edit Widgets. It comes in three sizes and fits itself to them: one or two profiles get large limits with their reset times, up to four get a block each in the large size, and more get a row each. It only reads what cl_audit saves: no Keychain, no network.">i</i></h4>
        <div class="srow stacked"><span>Limits as</span>
          <div class="design-pane">
            <span class="switch small-switch" role="group" aria-label="Limits as">{designs.map(([k, n]) => <button type="button" data-widget-design={k}>{n}</button>)}</span>
            <div class="wg-stage" data-widget-prev></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="srow stacked"><span>Look <i class="idot" title="System: the Mac's own widget background, light or dark, tinted like the other widgets. Std, Black and Silver: cl_audit's faces, as in this window.">i</i></span>
          <span class="switch small-switch" role="group" aria-label="Look">{widgetPlates.map(([k, n]) => <button type="button" data-widget-plate={k}>{n}</button>)}</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="g-col" data-gcol="3">
      <div class="colhead">The Dock</div>
      <!-- cl_audit's own icon: six designs in one sunken row, then one pane with Front panel's choices or Dock sizes -->
      <div class="card" data-appicon data-gcard="appicon"><h4>App icon</h4>
        <div class="ai-row6">
          {appIconStyles.map(([k, n]) => (
            <button type="button" class:list={["ai-t6", { on: k === "panel" }]} data-ai-style={k} title={`${n} app icon`}>
              <img src={appIconSrc({ ...appIconDefault, style: k })} data-ai-img={k} alt="" width="36" height="36" loading="lazy" /><span>{n}</span>
            </button>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="ai-pane" data-ai-lane></div>
        <p class="g-note">The cl_audit icon in the Dock and the app switcher, so it stands apart from the profile Dock icons. While cl_audit isn't running, the Dock shows the plain Front panel.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" data-gcard="dock"><h4>Dock</h4>
        <div class="srow"><span>Keep in order</span><span class="ctl-pair"><button type="button" class="mswitch" role="switch" aria-checked="false" data-demo-switch aria-label="Keep the Dock in order"></button><span class="sw-t" data-demo-t>off</span></span></div>
        <!-- the EXPERIMENTAL display under the row, before the description (app build 211) -->
        <span class="disp amber xs exp-disp">Experimental</span>
        <p class="g-note sub">Pins the profile icons side by side, in profile order, with at most one Claude pin before them. Redone when you add or remove a profile.</p>
        <p class="g-note caution">⚠︎ Invasive: it rewrites your Dock's pin list and restarts the Dock. “Restore my Dock” puts back the pins saved before the first change. (A demo: nothing in your Dock changes.)</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="dialog-veil" data-udialog hidden>
    <div class="dialog" role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="un-title">
      <b id="un-title">Uninstall cl_audit</b>
      <p>In the app, this removes every Dock icon app and its pin, the background agent, saved usage, settings and caches, then moves cl_audit to the Trash. Per profile you choose whether its Claude Code folder (signed out first) and its own Claude window's data go too; <code>~/.claude</code> and the standard window are never deleted.</p>
      <p class="sub">This is a demo, so nothing is removed.</p>
      <div class="dialog-btns"><button type="button" class="pill small" data-udialog-close>OK</button></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Forget… on a standby account and Move to Trash… on a folder from before: each asks first, as the app's alert does -->
  <div class="dialog-veil" data-ask hidden>
    <div class="dialog" role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="ask-title">
      <b id="ask-title" data-ask-title></b>
      <p data-ask-text></p>
      <p class="sub">This is a demo, so nothing on your Mac changes.</p>
      <div class="dialog-btns"><button type="button" class="pill small" data-ask-no>Cancel</button><button type="button" class="pill small tint t-red" data-ask-yes></button></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="dialog-veil" data-dialog hidden>
    <div class="dialog" role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="rm-title">
      <b id="rm-title">Remove “<span data-text="name">home</span>”?</b>
      <p>In the app, this removes its Dock icon app and what macOS keeps for it: preferences, caches, its pin in the Dock, plus its saved usage, the alert marks cl_audit keeps for it and its Terminal scripts. “Delete its folders too” also signs its Claude Code folder out and deletes it, and its Claude window's own folder.</p>
      <p class="sub">This is a demo, so nothing is removed here.</p>
      <div class="dialog-btns"><button type="button" class="pill small" data-dialog-close>OK</button></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
